4736328125, 0.0394287109375, 0.00472259521484375, 0.0186767578125, -0.0201873779296875, -0.01800537109375, -0.002895355224609375, 0.018218994140625, -0.00864410400390625, -0.0013551712036132812, 0.03826904296875, 0.01222991943359375, -0.0006928443908691406, 0.006412506103515625, 0.03656005859375, 0.00608062744140625, 0.004425048828125, -0.02325439453125, 0.0015249252319335938, -0.004245758056640625, -0.0268707275390625, 0.029815673828125, -0.0079345703125, -0.011383056640625, 0.0035305023193359375, 0.004581451416015625, 0.004405975341796875, 0.012359619140625, 0.0157928466796875, 0.01320648193359375, -0.0073699951171875, -0.011810302734375, -0.00878143310546875, -0.012451171875, -0.006839752197265625, -0.002315521240234375, 0.016510009765625, 0.0037841796875, 0.02069091796875, -0.021697998046875, -0.0080413818359375, -0.0162353515625, -0.01305389404296875, 0.014617919921875, -0.00426483154296875, 0.01335906982421875, -0.04473876953125, 0.0166015625, -0.0066986083984375, -0.0116729736328125, 0.0023021697998046875, 0.002033233642578125, 0.00032258033752441406, 0.00047898292541503906], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**\n1. Introduction\n**\n\nWe present an extension to the permanental process model\u2014a probabilistic model for random configurations of points\u2014by incorporating explicit fixed effects (covariates). Permanental processes are a class of random point process models used to describe collections of points (such as events in time or space) with distinctive dependency structures that differ from independent or Poisson-like interactions. In particular, they model attractive dependencies and can capture higher-order, nontrivial interactions among points\u2014features not present in Poisson or Cox processes. However, existing permanental process models generally lack the ability to directly include covariate information, such as experimental conditions or systematic trends, limiting their interpretability and applicability. Conversely, models like generalized additive models or Cox processes allow for covariates but do not retain the unique dependence structure of the permanental process. Our contribution is to integrate explicit fixed effects into the permanental framework, allowing joint modeling of structured spatial/temporal dependence and interpretable covariate effects.\n\n**\n2. Model Specification\n**\n\nLet $\\mathcal{X}$, the observation domain (e.g., time or spatial region), index possible event locations. For a finite collection of observed points $S = \\{x_1, \\ldots, x_n\\} \\subset \\mathcal{X}$, each observed location $x_i$ also has an associated covariate feature vector $z_i \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$ (with $z_i$ notationally distinct from $x_i$ to avoid ambiguity\u2014$x$ denotes location, $z$ denotes associated features or covariates). The classical permanental process (Macchi 1975) defines the likelihood of observing $S$ as an unordered set, with dependencies encoded by a positive-definite kernel $k$ and an intensity function. In our extension, the (log-)intensity function at each event location $x$ with covariates $z$ is specified as:\n$$\n\\lambda(x, z) = z^\\top \\beta + f(x)\n$$\nwhere $\\beta \\in \\mathbb{R}^p$ encodes fixed effect coefficients for the covariates, and $f(\\cdot)$ is a flexible function defined on $\\mathcal{X}$, lying in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS) with kernel $k$. Here, $\\lambda(x, z)$ parametrizes the likelihood of the configuration, but does not represent a pure Poisson intensity; instead, it enters multiplicatively into the permanental process configuration probability:\n$$\nP(S) \\propto \\mathrm{per}\\left([k^*(x_i, x_j)]_{i, j=1}^n\\right) \\prod_{i=1}^n \\exp(\\lambda(x_i, z_i))\n$$\nwhere $\\mathrm{per}(\\cdot)$ denotes the matrix permanent, and $k^*$ is a modified kernel (defined below).\n\n**Priors and Identifiability:**  \nWe place a zero-mean Gaussian process prior on $f$, $f \\sim \\mathcal{GP}(0, k)$, which offers flexibility: $f$ can represent smooth nonlinear effects on the domain $\\mathcal{X}$, adapting to complex spatial or temporal patterns as supported by data. This prior induces a penalization that prevents overfitting by discouraging excessive complexity; the kernel $k$ controls the smoothness and flexibility of $f$ in practice. The fixed effect coefficients $\\beta$ are assigned a Gaussian prior, $\\beta \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, \\tau^2 I_p)$. The diffuse prior limit ($\\tau^2\\to\\infty$) treats $\\beta$ as truly fixed/unpenalized, but we recommend using large, finite $\\tau^2$ to avoid identifiability and overfitting problems\u2014especially in finite samples or in the presence of collinear covariates. Practitioners may regularize more aggressively (with smaller $\\tau^2$) for high-dimensional $z_i$ or potentially correlated features.\n\n**\n3. Methodology\n**\n\n**3.1 Model Fitting and Modified Kernel Construction**\n\nOur approach fits $(\\beta, f)$ jointly by maximizing the penalized log-likelihood:\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}(\\beta, f) = -\\log P(S \\mid f, \\beta) + \\frac{1}{2} \\|f\\|_k^2 + \\frac{1}{2\\tau^2} \\|\\beta\\|^2\n\\]\nwhere $\\|f\\|_k$ denotes the RKHS norm induced by kernel $k$.\n\nBecause both fixed and smooth effects are present, identifiability of $f$ and $\\beta$ can be compromised\u2014especially if $f$ can represent the same variation as $\\beta^\\top z$, or vice versa. To resolve this, we construct a modified kernel $k^*$ so that the nonparametric part $f$ is orthogonal (in the data) to the span of the covariate functions. Formally:\n\nLet $Z = [z_1^\\top;\\ldots;z_n^\\top]$ (the $n \\times p$ covariate matrix), and $K$ the $n \\times n$ kernel Gram matrix $[k(x_i,x_j)]$. The projection matrix onto the covariate space is $P_Z = Z(Z^\\top Z)^{-1}Z^\\top$, projecting onto the column space of $Z$. The modified Gram matrix is\n\\[\nK^* = (I - P_Z) K (I - P_Z)\n\\]\nThis ensures that any function $f^*$ represented in the kernel span via $K^*$ satisfies $Z^\\top f^* = 0$ (i.e., is uncorrelated with the fixed effect directions in the observed data), so the fixed effects and nonparametric effects are distinguishable. Intuitively, $k^*$ prevents $f$ from \"leaking\" into linear trends expressible by $z^\\top\\beta$; this division of labor underpins identifiability and interpretability.\n\nWith this construction, the representer theorem confirms that the optimal $f$ is expressible as\n\\[\nf(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k^*(x, x_i)\n\\]\nand the log-intensity is\n\\[\n\\lambda(x, z) = z^\\top\\hat{\\beta} + \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k^*(x, x_i)\n\\]\n\n**Worked Example:**  \nSuppose we observe $n=3$ time events at locations $x_1, x_2, x_3$ with binary group indicators $z_i \\in \\{0,1\\}$. The GP kernel $k$ measures smoothness in time (e.g., squared exponential), $K$ is the $3\\times 3$ Gram matrix. To construct $K^*$, form $Z = [(z_1);(z_2);(z_3)]$, compute $P_Z$, and calculate $K^* = (I-P_Z)K(I-P_Z)$. After fitting, the estimated $f$ is orthogonal to group mean, and $\\hat{\\beta}$ captures the (regularized) mean difference between groups.\n\n**Estimation Algorithm:**\n1. Compute $K$ and $P_Z$, then $K^*$.\n2. Initialize $(\\beta, \\alpha)$, then optimize penalized likelihood (above) via, e.g., Newton or quasi-Newton methods.\n3. Recover $f(x)$ and $\\lambda(x, z)$ as above.\n\n**3.2 Comparison to Related Models**\n\nStandard generalized additive or Cox process models yield additive or smooth covariate effects for Poisson rate functions, but lack the rich interaction structure modeled by the permanental process' permanent-based probability. Our extended model supports both explicit covariate interpretation and the unique \u201cclumping/repulsion\" effects of permanental processes.\n\n**3.3 Practical and Theoretical Considerations**\n\n- **Covariate design:** $Z$ must be full column rank for unique estimation of $\\beta$; otherwise, fixed effects are non-identifiable even under regularization (see Hastie et al., 2009, Ch. 3).\n- **Measurement error:** Standard errors-in-variables effects (Carroll et al., 2006) bias $\\beta$ estimates; similar effects arise here\u2014methods such as regression calibration or SIMEX are applicable.\n- **Regularization:** Too large $\\tau^2$ in finite samples may yield overfitting or instability; cross-validation or information criteria should guide $\\tau^2$ choice.\n\n**\n4. Computation and Scalability\n**\n\nComputing the permanent $\\mathrm{per}(A)$ is \\#P-hard for general $A$. For practical inference:\n\n- **Ryser\u2019s algorithm**: Feasible for $n \\lesssim 20$ with dense kernels; for small datasets (e.g., $n \\leq 15$), runtimes are typically seconds to minutes on standard hardware.\n- **Importance sampling/MCMC/Laplace methods**: Effective for $n$ up to $\\sim 30$\u2013$50$ with sparse or structured $K^*$. Simulation studies (see Supplement C, Table 1) show, e.g., $n=30$ yields computation times around 3\u20135 minutes.\n- **Approximate permanent algorithms** (Clifford & Cosma, 2019): Allow $n$ in the 50\u2013100 range if $K^*$ is sparse; runtime rises rapidly with $n$.\n- **Determinantal (DPP) approximations**: Useful for rapid screening but may misrepresent positive"}}